---
// The printed-sheet treatment: a CMYK rosette screen plus paper grain, laid
// over the whole page. Figma does this with a viewport-sized `Sunset 001 3`
// rectangle at SOFT_LIGHT on top of every frame.
//
// The layers stay viewport-fixed and ui.js drives `--print-y` from the scroll
// position instead, so the texture travels with the content like one continuous
// sheet while the composited layer is never taller than the screen. A
// document-tall blend layer would be the obvious build and is the expensive one.
//
// Three ROOT-LEVEL siblings rather than three children of one wrapper: an
// element with `mix-blend-mode` blends against its backdrop, and that backdrop
// is clipped to the nearest stacking-context ancestor. A `fixed` + `z-index`
// wrapper is a stacking context, so nesting these inside one would isolate them
// into an empty group and silently reduce every blend mode to `normal` — the
// layers must sit directly in the root stacking context to see the page under
// them. Each needs its own mode (overlay for the screen and the stock tint,
// soft-light for the grain), which is why it is three elements and not one with
// stacked backgrounds.
import rosette from "../assets/textures/rosette.png";
---

<div
    id="print-screen"
    aria-hidden="true"
    class="print-screen pointer-events-none fixed inset-0 z-[60] saturate-120"
    style={`background-image:url(${rosette.src})`}
>
</div>
<div
    id="print-tint"
    aria-hidden="true"
    class="print-tint pointer-events-none fixed inset-0 z-[61]"
>
</div>
<div
    id="print-grain"
    aria-hidden="true"
    class="print-grain pointer-events-none fixed inset-0 z-[62]"
>
</div>
